              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_16_table_6.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tbody><tr><td>Match</td><td>Match1</td><td>Match2</td><td>Match3</td><td>Match4</td><td>Match5</td><td>Match6</td><td>Match7</td><td>Match8</td></tr><tr><td>Runs</td><td>80</td><td>50</td><td>10</td><td>100</td><td>90</td><td>0</td><td>90</td><td>50</td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.706,
            0.887,
            0.867
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In this example, the minimum score is 0 and the maximum score is 100. Using a scale of 1 unit length = l run would mean that we have to go all the way from 0 to 100 runs in steps of l. This would be unnecessarily tedious. Instead, let us use a scale where 1 unit length = 10 runs. We mark this scale on the vertical line and draw the bars according to the scores in each match. We get the following bar graph representing the above data."
            }
          ]
        },
